  <h2>Where AI helps in electrical engineering</h2>
  <p>Electrical engineers get the most from AI on <strong>NEC lookup</strong>, <strong>panel schedules and one-line diagrams</strong>, <strong>lighting layouts</strong>, and <strong>documentation</strong>. AI is not a substitute for your short-circuit, coordination, and arc-flash tools (SKM, ETAP, EasyPower, Paladin) and it does not stamp drawings. Used at the front of the workflow, it removes the most tedious drafting hours from senior engineers' plates.</p>

  <p><em>Official references:</em> <a href="https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/nfpa-70-standard-development" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">NFPA 70 (NEC) Standard Development</a> · <a href="https://www.ies.org/standards/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Illuminating Engineering Society Standards</a> · <a href="https://www.ieee.org/standards/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">IEEE Standards Association</a></p>

  <p><img src="/images/blog/ai-tools-for-electrical-engineers-2026/workflow.svg" alt="Electrical engineering AI workflow: NEC lookup, panel schedules, short-circuit analysis" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></p>

  <p>For the cross-discipline view, see the <a href="/solutions/ai-for-engineers/">AI tools for engineers</a> hub and the <a href="/solutions/ai-for-electrical-engineers/">electrical engineering solutions</a> page. The <a href="/solutions/ai-for-mep-engineers/">AI for MEP engineers</a> page covers the wider MEP context.</p>

  <h2>NEC code lookup with citations</h2>
  <p>The most expensive use of an electrical engineer's time is the multi-tab hunt through NFPA-70. AI flips that into a 30-second Q&amp;A session, but only if you ground the model in the actual standard.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Load NFPA-70 (NEC), NFPA-70E, and the relevant IEEE color books into a <a href="/tool/dify/">Dify</a> RAG, chunked by article and section with citations.</li>
    <li>Ask narrow questions: "Minimum conductor size for a 100A feeder, 75°C terminals, copper, three-phase."</li>
    <li>Read the cited articles back. If the citation does not point to a real article, treat the answer as a draft.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <a href="/workflows/dify-rag-document-qna/">Dify RAG for document Q&amp;A</a> workflow has the setup. For general drafting, <a href="/tool/claude/">Claude</a> is the strongest general-purpose LLM for long NEC documents; see the <a href="/vs/chatgpt-vs-claude/">ChatGPT vs Claude</a> page for the trade-off.</p>

  <h2>Panel schedules and one-line diagrams</h2>
  <p>AI is most useful for first-pass panel schedules and one-line diagrams, but the engineer must verify every connection and protective device coordination before the drawing issues:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use <a href="/tool/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> to generate the panel schedule from a load list, including bus rating, main breaker, and feeder sizes.</li>
    <li>Use <a href="/tool/claude/">Claude</a> to read the long load study and surface unusual loads, single-phase imbalances, or coordination conflicts.</li>
    <li>For one-line diagrams, AI generates a draft from an equipment schedule. The engineer reviews connectivity, protective device settings, and safety labeling before the drawing goes out.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For panel schedule automation in the office, <a href="/tool/n8n/">n8n</a> wires your spreadsheet or Revit schedule to the LLM and pushes drafts back to the project. The <a href="/workflows/n8n-webhook-automation/">n8n webhook automation</a> workflow is the right starting point.</p>

  <h2>Lighting design</h2>
  <p>AI generates a useful first layout based on room geometry, IES recommendations, and the project's design criteria. The actual foot-candle calculation and aiming still need an engineer's tool:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use <a href="/tool/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> to draft the lighting layout based on room dimensions, target illuminance, and fixture types.</li>
    <li>Verify with AGi32, Visual, or your firm's lighting design software. The numbers from the model are starting points, not stampable output.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Arc flash, short-circuit, and coordination</h2>
  <p>AI assists with data collection and report drafting; the analysis itself stays in your solver of record:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use <a href="/tool/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> to draft the data-collection checklist and the arc-flash label templates.</li>
    <li>Run the analysis in SKM Power Tools, ETAP, EasyPower, or Paladin. These tools use verified system data; an LLM cannot reproduce the protective device coordination logic safely.</li>
    <li>Use AI to draft the report text and the executive summary once the analysis is complete.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What AI will not do for you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sealed calculations and panel schedules.</strong> The PE seal covers the final design and the equipment selection, and that means the engineer has personally verified every connection and every protective device setting.</li>
    <li><strong>NEC citations as a fact.</strong> Treat any code paragraph the model produces as a draft, not authority. Look up the actual article.</li>
    <li><strong>Short-circuit and coordination analysis.</strong> Run those in your solver of record, not in the LLM.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Recommended 30-day rollout</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> Stand up a Dify RAG over NFPA-70 and NFPA-70E. Pilot on one project.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> Add Claude for long report summarisation and ChatGPT for panel schedule drafts. Lock a "no final sizes in chat" rule.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> Wire n8n into your panel schedule workflow. Measure drafting time before and after.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Document the firm's working stack, train junior staff, and update QA/QC to call out which AI outputs require a PE's review.</li>
  </ol>
